# C++ unordered_map Cheatsheet

`#include <unordered_map>`

Hash map: average O(1) insert/lookup/erase, O(n) worst case.
For ordered keys or guaranteed log n, use `map` instead.

## Declare
```cpp
unordered_map<string, int> mp;
unordered_map<char, vector<string>> mp;
unordered_map<int, int> mp = {{1, 10}, {2, 20}};
```

## Insert / update
```cpp
mp["key"] = 5;                    // insert or overwrite
mp.insert({"key", 5});            // insert only if key doesn't exist
mp["counter"]++;                  // auto-creates with default (0) if missing
mp["list"].push_back(x);          // auto-creates empty vector if missing
```

## Lookup
```cpp
mp["key"]                         // returns value, CREATES entry if missing (default-init)
mp.at("key")                      // returns value, THROWS if missing (safer for reads)
mp.count("key")                   // returns 1 if present, 0 if not
mp.find("key")                    // returns iterator, mp.end() if missing
mp.contains("key")                // C++20, returns bool
```

## Check before access
```cpp
if (mp.count(key))         { /* present */ }
if (mp.find(key) != mp.end()) { /* present */ }
if (mp.contains(key))      { /* present, C++20 */ }
```

## Get with default if missing
```cpp
int val = mp.count(key) ? mp[key] : 0;
```

## Erase
```cpp
mp.erase("key");                  // by key
mp.erase(mp.find("key"));         // by iterator
mp.clear();                       // remove all
```

## Iterate
```cpp
for (auto& [k, v] : mp) {         // C++17 structured binding
    // k, v
}

for (auto& pair : mp) {           // old style
    // pair.first, pair.second
}
```

## Size / empty
```cpp
mp.size()      mp.empty()
```

## Common patterns

```cpp
// frequency count
unordered_map<char, int> freq;
for (char c : s) freq[c]++;

// grouping (anagrams, etc.)
unordered_map<string, vector<string>> groups;
for (auto& s : strs) groups[key(s)].push_back(s);

// last-seen index
unordered_map<char, int> lastSeen;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (lastSeen.count(s[i])) { /* seen before at lastSeen[s[i]] */ }
    lastSeen[s[i]] = i;
}

// two-sum complement lookup
unordered_map<int, int> seen;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    int need = target - nums[i];
    if (seen.count(need)) return {seen[need], i};
    seen[nums[i]] = i;
}
```

## Gotchas

- `mp["missing"]` CREATES the entry with default value. Use `count` or `find` for read-only checks if you don't want side effects.
- Default values: int=0, double=0.0, string="", vector={}, pointer=nullptr.
- No ordering. Iteration order is unspecified.
- Keys must be hashable. Built-in types and `string` work out of the box. Custom types need a custom hash.

## unordered_map vs map

| | unordered_map | map |
|---|---|---|
| backing | hash table | red-black tree |
| ordered keys | no | yes (sorted) |
| insert / lookup | avg O(1), worst O(n) | O(log n) guaranteed |
| use when | speed, no order needed | need sorted iteration or guaranteed log n |

## unordered_set (related)

`#include <unordered_set>` — same hash table without values, for membership checks.

```cpp
unordered_set<int> seen;
seen.insert(x);
if (seen.count(x)) { ... }
seen.erase(x);
```
